1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,161
Hi, I'm Suketh Produtoor.

2
00:00:02,181 --> 00:00:06,636
I'm an international student on F-1, looking for new-grad machine learning roles.

3
00:00:06,656 --> 00:00:09,454
The question isn't only whether a company sponsors.

4
00:00:09,474 --> 00:00:11,553
It's whether the posting even fits me.

5
00:00:11,573 --> 00:00:15,705
Earlier this term, Claude Code and I built a recipe for the course's job-search

6
00:00:15,725 --> 00:00:17,386
engine, the Reallocation Engine.

7
00:00:17,406 --> 00:00:21,801
It checks a company's sponsor record, its past work-visa filings, at the role level.

8
00:00:21,821 --> 00:00:24,954
Claude wrote the code; I chose the rules and ran the checks.

9
00:00:24,974 --> 00:00:26,636
Its thirty-six tests still pass.

10
00:00:26,656 --> 00:00:30,767
Then it screened eight real postings by title and company record, for Arjun Rao,

11
00:00:30,787 --> 00:00:33,401
a fictional applicant with no work history on file.

12
00:00:33,421 --> 00:00:36,861
Read in full, six asked for two to twelve-plus years of experience,

13
00:00:36,881 --> 00:00:38,204
or excluded new graduates.

14
00:00:38,224 --> 00:00:41,612
An AI reviewer read them, Claude re-checked, and I held all eight.

15
00:00:41,632 --> 00:00:44,628
A relevant title and a sponsor record are useful signals.

16
00:00:44,648 --> 00:00:47,114
But the posting itself can change the decision.

17
00:00:47,134 --> 00:00:50,553
So I will build Fine Print: an agent that will read each posting,

18
00:00:50,573 --> 00:00:52,881
quote the sentence behind every requirement,

19
00:00:52,901 --> 00:00:56,161
and stop for my approval before anything reaches my shortlist.

20
00:00:56,181 --> 00:00:59,558
Every requirement it finds will need its sentence, word for word.

21
00:00:59,578 --> 00:01:02,641
Code will check that the sentence really is in the posting.

22
00:01:02,661 --> 00:01:04,993
If it isn't, the requirement gets thrown out.

23
00:01:05,013 --> 00:01:07,596
What could sink it: a real quote, read the wrong way.

24
00:01:07,616 --> 00:01:11,132
Say a posting says five years preferred, and the agent records required.

25
00:01:11,152 --> 00:01:12,752
The quote check would still pass.

26
00:01:12,772 --> 00:01:15,699
So preferred, required, and either-or rules will stay apart,

27
00:01:15,719 --> 00:01:17,516
and anything unclear will stop at me.

28
00:01:17,536 --> 00:01:21,342
A requirement also needs the applicant's documented background beside it.

29
00:01:21,362 --> 00:01:24,958
I have about two and a half years at Amazon and six months at Vertex,

30
00:01:24,978 --> 00:01:27,788
so two years required needs a comparison, not a guess.

31
00:01:27,808 --> 00:01:30,828
The repo will use a fictional profile; mine stays private.

32
00:01:30,848 --> 00:01:34,875
It will run as a loop: fetch a public posting from an allowed job board, read it,

33
00:01:34,895 --> 00:01:37,073
check the sponsor record, log what it found.

34
00:01:37,093 --> 00:01:39,971
It will stop when the list is done or the budget runs out.

35
00:01:39,991 --> 00:01:42,369
It will read, and never apply, email, or log in.

36
00:01:42,389 --> 00:01:46,171
That's the human gate: nothing will reach my shortlist until I approve it,

37
00:01:46,191 --> 00:01:48,432
and the record will keep exactly what I saw.

38
00:01:48,452 --> 00:01:51,310
A value the model proposed will still say source: model,

39
00:01:51,330 --> 00:01:54,444
and my approval will be recorded beside it, as its own field.

40
00:01:54,464 --> 00:01:56,705
My earlier prototype labelled those as mine.

41
00:01:56,725 --> 00:02:00,525
I checked graduate ML descriptions from Qualcomm and TikTok on LinkedIn.

42
00:02:00,545 --> 00:02:03,230
Both separate minimum and preferred qualifications;

43
00:02:03,250 --> 00:02:05,883
neither accessible description states sponsorship.

44
00:02:05,903 --> 00:02:09,808
Fine Print will preserve those distinctions, attach checked source quotes,

45
00:02:09,828 --> 00:02:12,620
and keep missing sponsorship evidence marked unknown.

46
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:13,343
Who opens it?

47
00:02:13,363 --> 00:02:15,291
A hiring manager or an ML engineer.

48
00:02:15,311 --> 00:02:18,686
In the first minute, they'll run one command with no API key,

49
00:02:18,706 --> 00:02:22,692
which replays recorded model outputs, and read the report: each posting,

50
00:02:22,712 --> 00:02:25,697
each requirement beside its sentence, and my decision.

51
00:02:25,717 --> 00:02:29,073
It should prove I can build an agent whose claims can be checked.

52
00:02:29,093 --> 00:02:32,968
I'll label real postings by hand, set a held-out group aside before tuning,

53
00:02:32,988 --> 00:02:36,811
and count two mistakes separately: clearing a posting I don't qualify for,

54
00:02:36,831 --> 00:02:38,006
and excluding one I do.

55
00:02:38,026 --> 00:02:39,421
It will never apply for me.

56
00:02:39,441 --> 00:02:43,563
The first piece will be one loop over one public posting, with the quote check,

57
00:02:43,583 --> 00:02:46,603
and a test that fails if a quote changes by a single word.

58
00:02:46,623 --> 00:02:47,486
Why this project?

59
00:02:47,506 --> 00:02:48,058
It's a mix.

60
00:02:48,078 --> 00:02:50,188
I already have production ML from Amazon,

61
00:02:50,208 --> 00:02:53,150
data checks with human sign-off from Vertex, and AgentOS,

62
00:02:53,170 --> 00:02:56,268
my open-source project with budget limits and a kill switch.

63
00:02:56,288 --> 00:02:59,593
New to me: reading a requirement without changing what it means,

64
00:02:59,613 --> 00:03:02,347
and testing extraction and interpretation separately.

65
00:03:02,367 --> 00:03:06,184
Today there's the recipe, its passing tests, the eight-posting finding,

66
00:03:06,204 --> 00:03:07,534
and an open pull request.

67
00:03:07,554 --> 00:03:10,344
Not yet: the agent, the quote check, the evaluation.

68
00:03:10,364 --> 00:03:11,371
Those I will build.

69
00:03:11,391 --> 00:03:12,151
Fine Print.

70
00:03:12,171 --> 00:03:13,782
Suketh Reddy Produtoor.
